i model to predict whether or not a  defendant will reoffend if they're  released from jail  um and and what a lot of people found is  that you know  people often don't trust the ai tool so  we're trying to figure out why  um and a lot of times it's because  people see this noise  and they think it's the signal and we as  statisticians are  as data scientists we get noise we're  like oh that was just  this random thing we know randomness but  um  people in the real world these  experiences of seeing something happen  might not translate into a tool  you know that we want to be useful  so what things impact algorithmic trust  well according to abdullah al matuka mit  there's a lot of different things  transparency how well does the algorithm  work  interpretability can the model explain  its own decisions in a ways that humans  can understand performance is it  communicated well is it performing well  and control can the user actually modify  the algorithm  in our case i would say we fail in all  three except for one case  we do we do communicate the performance  well with our visual  but we're really not giving people very  much control and we're definitely not  letting them inside the black box of  this algorithm  so what we're planning on to do planning  on doing next is  giving them a little more uh insight  into this black box  so we're going to add these local  interpretability measures at the project  level where you can see  for example for this project um why does  the prediction  give us the result it does and it gives  you a breakdown by  um by variable it's not quite as  straightforward as saying a linear  regression model but  it gives you some insight into the black  box  of why a particular project has its  prediction  brighter model so what are the key  takeaways  first when designing a live prediction  model stay organized  make it flexible so one error doesn't  break the whole thing  and document your entire data flow so  this is the very  unsexy ta